  "result": "I opened the issue: https://github.com/acme/textstats/issues/13\n\nI ran it to confirm: `word_count(\"\")` returns `1`. The cause is in `textstats/__init__.py:6`, which splits on a literal space (`text.split(\" \")`). An empty string split that way still gives a one-item list. The same split causes a related problem: text that is only spaces gets the wrong count, so `\"   \"` returns `4`.\n\nThe issue includes:\n- steps to reproduce\n- the cause\n- the expected behavior\n- a note that `average_word_length` in the same file already uses `text.split()` (no argument), which handles these cases\n- a note that the tests have no case for empty input\n\nI didn't change any code.",
